WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.180
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.100
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.020
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:14.830
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.520
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.650
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.500
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.889 --> 00:00:25.240
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's the

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.210
start of another three-part mini-series,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.509
this time on unusual British traditions.

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:34.739
In today's episode, part one, we are

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.570
going to be talking about Punch and Judy,

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.869
the strange, violent, and originally

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.590
Italian puppet show that became one of

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.420
Britain’s best-known seaside traditions.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.900
In part two, we turn to fox hunting,

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.220
a very different type of tradition

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.629
that is technically illegal, but

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.040
still goes on up and down the country.

00:00:57.389 --> 00:00:59.529
And in part three, we are going to

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.120
be talking about the rise and fall

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.430
of the British seaside holiday.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.110
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:09.780
minute and get right into it.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.510
Let me start this episode by

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.100
teaching you a completely pointless

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:17.800
piece of English vocabulary.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.110
It's a word I almost guarantee

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.989
you've never heard, and that applies

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.530
even if you are a native speaker.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.230
That word is a swazzle.

00:01:31.796 --> 00:01:35.430
S-W-A-Z-Z-L-E.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.780
It's a small metal device you can put

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.389
in your mouth that turns your voice

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.270
into something so unusual I don't think

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.169
I'm even going to try to describe it.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.699
I'll just play it for you:

00:01:54.997 --> 00:01:57.507
That is the voice of a character called

00:01:57.537 --> 00:02:00.827
Mr Punch, the star of the strange British

00:02:00.827 --> 00:02:04.017
tradition of the Punch and Judy show.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:07.487
Now, Punch and Judy is one of those

00:02:07.487 --> 00:02:10.137
things that feels deeply, perhaps

00:02:10.137 --> 00:02:12.157
even quintessentially, British.

00:02:12.517 --> 00:02:14.427
A small red-and-white

00:02:14.437 --> 00:02:16.417
striped booth on a beach.

00:02:16.757 --> 00:02:19.417
A hook-nosed puppet with a squeaky

00:02:19.417 --> 00:02:22.251
voice, a stick in his hand, and a

00:02:22.354 --> 00:02:24.817
crowd of children laughing along.

00:02:25.527 --> 00:02:28.037
It feels about as British as fish

00:02:28.037 --> 00:02:30.797
and chips, wet deckchairs and

00:02:30.827 --> 00:02:32.307
disappointing summer weather.

00:02:33.217 --> 00:02:36.017
It is also, in its origins, not

00:02:36.037 --> 00:02:38.497
really British at all; it's Italian.

00:02:39.147 --> 00:02:42.047
So, let's start with where it comes

00:02:42.047 --> 00:02:44.287
from, and how it got to Britain.

00:02:45.637 --> 00:02:48.387
In the early seventeenth century, in the

00:02:48.387 --> 00:02:51.677
streets and squares of Naples, something

00:02:51.677 --> 00:02:54.417
called commedia dell'arte was flourishing.

00:02:55.337 --> 00:02:57.887
Now, commedia dell'arte was a kind of

00:02:58.397 --> 00:03:01.277
improvised street comedy, performed by

00:03:01.327 --> 00:03:03.707
travelling players who would arrive in

00:03:03.707 --> 00:03:06.937
a town, set up a stage, and entertain

00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:09.737
crowds with rude jokes, slapstick

00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:12.207
music, and stock characters that

00:03:12.247 --> 00:03:14.407
everyone in the audience already knew.

00:03:15.647 --> 00:03:18.077
One of those stock characters

00:03:18.387 --> 00:03:20.657
was a man called Pulcinella.

00:03:21.547 --> 00:03:24.347
He was a Neapolitan invention, and he

00:03:24.347 --> 00:03:28.007
had a hooked nose and a hump on his back.

00:03:29.077 --> 00:03:33.217
He wore a white costume, a black mask, and

00:03:33.527 --> 00:03:37.547
he spoke in a strange, squawking voice, a

00:03:37.547 --> 00:03:39.387
bit like the one you heard a minute ago.

00:03:40.797 --> 00:03:42.227
He wasn't a nice man.

00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:44.717
He was cunning, lazy,

00:03:45.147 --> 00:03:47.597
greedy, and easily upset.

00:03:48.247 --> 00:03:49.307
He hit people.

00:03:49.507 --> 00:03:52.147
He insulted figures of authority.

00:03:52.577 --> 00:03:54.777
He was the sort of character someone in

00:03:54.777 --> 00:03:57.127
seventeenth-century Naples wasn't meant

00:03:57.137 --> 00:03:59.507
to admire, but would laugh at anyway.

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:03.307
The name Pulcinella is thought to

00:04:03.307 --> 00:04:04.867
have come from the Italian word

00:04:05.122 --> 00:04:07.557
"pulcino", which means "little chick",

00:04:07.617 --> 00:04:10.277
baby chicken, so it's a reference

00:04:10.397 --> 00:04:13.107
to his beaky, chicken-like face

00:04:13.127 --> 00:04:16.087
and funny, almost bird-like voice.

00:04:17.017 --> 00:04:19.427
Now, Pulcinella was a hit

00:04:19.657 --> 00:04:21.367
in 17th century Naples.

00:04:21.797 --> 00:04:24.747
And like all hits, he travelled.

00:04:25.787 --> 00:04:27.807
Italian performers took him to

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:30.501
Paris, where he became Polichinelle.

00:04:30.847 --> 00:04:32.617
They took him to other European cities,

00:04:32.917 --> 00:04:35.837
where his name was tweaked or translated.

00:04:36.591 --> 00:04:39.357
And on the 9th of May

00:04:39.753 --> 00:04:42.957
1662, he came to London.

00:04:44.127 --> 00:04:45.967
We know that date because of

00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:48.213
a man called Samuel Pepys.

00:04:49.113 --> 00:04:51.367
Pepys is one of the most useful

00:04:51.377 --> 00:04:52.797
figures in English history.

00:04:53.557 --> 00:04:56.437
For nearly a decade he kept a

00:04:56.477 --> 00:04:58.737
detailed personal diary that

00:04:58.737 --> 00:05:01.557
recorded everything he ate, drank,

00:05:01.957 --> 00:05:04.147
gossiped about, and went to see.

00:05:04.987 --> 00:05:07.937
And an extra little fun fact about

00:05:07.937 --> 00:05:09.957
Samuel Pepys is that he wrote one

00:05:09.957 --> 00:05:11.997
of the most detailed accounts of the

00:05:11.997 --> 00:05:14.217
Great Fire of London, which happened

00:05:14.217 --> 00:05:17.917
in 1666, and as the flames approached

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:21.387
his house, he dug a big hole to protect

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.897
his most prized possessions, the first

00:05:23.897 --> 00:05:26.037
of which was his parmesan cheese.

00:05:27.477 --> 00:05:30.327
And as one more side note, we do have

00:05:30.327 --> 00:05:32.267
an episode on The Great Fire of London.

00:05:32.537 --> 00:05:34.067
It's one of our member-only ones,

00:05:34.107 --> 00:05:36.661
and it's episode number 115.

00:05:37.197 --> 00:05:39.387
Anyway, back to the story.

00:05:40.017 --> 00:05:43.097
On that particular Friday in May

00:05:43.157 --> 00:05:46.987
1662, Samuel Pepys walked through

00:05:47.027 --> 00:05:49.117
Covent Garden in central London

00:05:49.257 --> 00:05:51.787
and stopped to watch a puppet show.

00:05:52.747 --> 00:05:54.957
The puppeteer was an Italian

00:05:55.227 --> 00:05:56.994
known as Signor Bologna.

00:05:58.117 --> 00:06:00.127
He was performing a marionette show,

00:06:00.467 --> 00:06:02.727
which is a show with stringed puppets,

00:06:03.107 --> 00:06:07.357
and one of his characters was a hook-nosed

00:06:07.437 --> 00:06:10.687
troublemaker called Punchinello, the

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:13.367
Anglicised version of Pulcinella.

00:06:14.597 --> 00:06:16.737
Pepys was impressed.

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:19.387
He described the show as "very

00:06:19.407 --> 00:06:21.317
pretty, the best that ever I saw,

00:06:21.517 --> 00:06:23.861
and a great resort of gallants."

00:06:24.627 --> 00:06:26.497
A great resort of gallants is an

00:06:26.577 --> 00:06:28.777
unusual phrase, and not something

00:06:28.777 --> 00:06:30.797
you'd hear nowadays, but it

00:06:30.797 --> 00:06:33.177
just means a fashionable crowd.

00:06:33.807 --> 00:06:35.547
In other words, it was a show

00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:37.457
for adults, not children.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.290
Within months, Pepys

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:42.280
had seen the show again.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.870
And he was not the only fan.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:48.270
Later that same year, King Charles

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:50.910
II, the recently restored king

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.700
of England, asked Signor Bologna

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.360
to perform for him at his palace.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:59.310
The king was so delighted that he

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:02.110
gave the puppeteer a gold chain and

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:05.210
medal worth twenty-five pounds, which

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:06.990
works out at something like five

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:09.360
thousand Euros in today's money.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:13.955
That date, the 9th of May 1662,

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.050
is now traditionally treated

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.610
as Mr Punch's UK birthday.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.530
There is even a small festival held

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:24.030
in Covent Garden each May to mark it.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.340
But the character that Pepys saw

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:30.400
that afternoon was still, in every

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:32.780
meaningful sense, an Italian visitor.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.240
The journey from Italian puppet

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.370
to British icon would take the

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.190
best part of two centuries.

00:07:41.387 --> 00:07:43.107
The first thing that needed to

00:07:43.107 --> 00:07:45.637
change was the puppet itself.

00:07:46.437 --> 00:07:48.880
The original Mr Punch, Pulcinella,

00:07:49.177 --> 00:07:51.497
was a marionette: a puppet on

00:07:51.497 --> 00:07:53.847
strings, operated from above.

00:07:54.987 --> 00:07:57.347
Putting on a marionette show requires

00:07:57.387 --> 00:08:00.327
a large stage and quite a lot of kit.

00:08:00.977 --> 00:08:03.257
A glove puppet, on the other hand,

00:08:03.557 --> 00:08:06.327
sits directly on the performer's hand.

00:08:06.847 --> 00:08:09.067
The puppeteer stands hidden inside

00:08:09.067 --> 00:08:11.657
a simple vertical fabric booth,

00:08:11.977 --> 00:08:13.947
holds his hands above his head, and

00:08:13.977 --> 00:08:16.217
operates the puppets from below.

00:08:16.917 --> 00:08:19.177
The booth can be a single striped

00:08:19.217 --> 00:08:21.697
canvas sheet thrown over a frame.

00:08:22.257 --> 00:08:24.127
The whole thing is very light,

00:08:24.497 --> 00:08:26.657
and can be set up and packed

00:08:26.657 --> 00:08:28.857
away in a matter of minutes.

00:08:29.837 --> 00:08:32.937
Whatsmore, glove puppets move in a

00:08:32.967 --> 00:08:36.058
completely different way to marionettes.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.417
Marionettes move gracefully, slowly,

00:08:39.517 --> 00:08:41.317
like they're floating in the air.

00:08:42.187 --> 00:08:44.167
Glove puppets are different.

00:08:44.337 --> 00:08:46.347
Everything is more physical.

00:08:46.497 --> 00:08:48.257
You can throw them around.

00:08:48.267 --> 00:08:50.327
You can have one whack another

00:08:50.517 --> 00:08:52.323
over the head with a thud.

00:08:52.584 --> 00:08:55.357
You can throw a baby out of a window.

00:08:55.527 --> 00:08:57.247
You can fight a crocodile,

00:08:57.467 --> 00:08:58.937
slapping it with a stick.

00:08:59.777 --> 00:09:03.157
In fact, the word "slapstick" comes

00:09:03.167 --> 00:09:05.737
from this world, as in "slapstick

00:09:05.787 --> 00:09:08.327
comedy", the style of exaggerated

00:09:08.327 --> 00:09:10.817
comedy where people bang into walls

00:09:10.817 --> 00:09:12.467
or pretend to hurt themselves.

00:09:13.377 --> 00:09:16.427
So at some point in the eighteenth

00:09:16.437 --> 00:09:19.207
century, Punch made the leap

00:09:19.387 --> 00:09:21.547
from one form to the other,

00:09:21.687 --> 00:09:24.437
from marionette to glove puppet.

00:09:25.657 --> 00:09:27.737
And this is when things

00:09:27.767 --> 00:09:29.367
really started to take off.

00:09:29.957 --> 00:09:33.017
A glove puppet show could be set up at the

00:09:33.037 --> 00:09:35.737
corner of a busy street, performed for an

00:09:35.747 --> 00:09:38.647
hour, and packed away in a wheelbarrow.

00:09:39.127 --> 00:09:42.327
The whole thing was portable, cheap, and

00:09:42.427 --> 00:09:44.987
perfectly suited to street performance.

00:09:45.807 --> 00:09:48.397
This new kind of show needed

00:09:48.407 --> 00:09:50.187
a new kind of performer.

00:09:51.457 --> 00:09:54.657
And it could all be operated by one

00:09:54.707 --> 00:09:57.177
person, who would stand inside the booth

00:09:57.387 --> 00:10:00.107
and operate the puppets with both hands.

00:10:01.147 --> 00:10:04.117
He became known as a "professor".

00:10:04.877 --> 00:10:07.897
Why "professor", nobody is quite sure.

00:10:08.417 --> 00:10:10.887
It seems to have been a slightly grand,

00:10:10.897 --> 00:10:13.587
slightly playful title, given to a

00:10:13.587 --> 00:10:15.787
man who had mastered a craft that was

00:10:15.807 --> 00:10:18.987
difficult and not entirely respectable.

00:10:20.007 --> 00:10:22.357
The professor also had a secret

00:10:22.407 --> 00:10:24.957
weapon, and it was the same secret

00:10:24.967 --> 00:10:27.137
weapon that Italian Pulcinella

00:10:27.397 --> 00:10:28.907
had used a century before.

00:10:29.597 --> 00:10:32.547
A small metal reed, held against the

00:10:32.547 --> 00:10:35.437
roof of the mouth, that gave Punch his

00:10:35.537 --> 00:10:39.227
unmistakable, ear-piercing, squeaky voice.

00:10:39.927 --> 00:10:42.097
In English, this device became

00:10:42.097 --> 00:10:45.327
known, as you now know, as a swazzle.

00:10:52.134 --> 00:10:54.105
[  , Now, I have to confess that I

00:10:54.115 --> 00:10:56.315
have never tried it, but it does

00:10:56.315 --> 00:10:58.405
appear that the swazzle is not

00:10:58.485 --> 00:11:00.535
an easy piece of kit to use.

00:11:01.135 --> 00:11:03.415
You need to shape the words clearly

00:11:03.695 --> 00:11:06.055
while forcing air through the reed,

00:11:06.385 --> 00:11:08.955
switching between Punch’s voice and your

00:11:08.965 --> 00:11:11.895
normal voice, all while making sure you

00:11:11.895 --> 00:11:13.835
don’t accidentally swallow the thing.

00:11:14.435 --> 00:11:17.855
But if you manage it, if you master

00:11:17.855 --> 00:11:19.975
it, you'll be able to produce something

00:11:20.215 --> 00:11:23.165
that sounds like a chicken arguing

00:11:23.175 --> 00:11:24.995
through a child's toy whistle.

00:11:25.915 --> 00:11:28.515
Punch professors trained for years

00:11:28.695 --> 00:11:31.345
to use one properly, and even today

00:11:31.565 --> 00:11:33.975
the swazzle is considered the badge

00:11:34.075 --> 00:11:36.315
of a real Punch and Judy performer.

00:11:36.735 --> 00:11:38.545
If you cannot do the voice, you are

00:11:38.545 --> 00:11:41.195
not accepted as a real Punch man.

00:11:42.475 --> 00:11:45.195
Punch also got a new wardrobe.

00:11:45.665 --> 00:11:47.885
The plain white Italian costume

00:11:48.115 --> 00:11:50.495
was dropped, and by the middle of

00:11:50.495 --> 00:11:52.895
the eighteenth century Punch had

00:11:52.895 --> 00:11:55.444
taken on the red and yellow colours

00:11:55.536 --> 00:11:58.025
of the English jester, with a tall

00:11:58.065 --> 00:12:00.735
pointy hat and a stick in his hand.

00:12:01.495 --> 00:12:04.135
He looked, in other words, less like a

00:12:04.135 --> 00:12:06.895
Neapolitan street performer and more like

00:12:06.895 --> 00:12:09.725
a figure from a medieval English court.

00:12:10.525 --> 00:12:12.855
He also gained a cast, supporting

00:12:12.855 --> 00:12:15.455
actors, or rather, supporting puppets.

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:18.490
And this is where the story

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:19.940
gets a bit complicated.

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:22.200
For a long time, there

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.040
was no fixed script.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.580
Each professor performed his own version

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.490
of the show, with his own characters,

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:32.510
his own jokes, and his own running order.

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:36.660
The most famous surviving version comes

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:39.360
from the late 1820s, when a writer

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:42.830
called John Payne Collier recorded, or

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:45.400
claimed to record, the performance of a

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:47.880
Punch performer named Giovanni Piccini.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:51.870
The book was illustrated by a celebrated

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.220
caricaturist and it was given a

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:57.120
wonderful title: "The Tragical Comedy

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.540
or Comical Tragedy of Punch and Judy".

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:04.920
This 1828 book became the

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:06.590
canonical version of the show.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:09.270
It is what most people today think

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:11.430
of as the "traditional" script.

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:14.010
There is, however, a

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:15.270
slight problem with it.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:17.870
John Payne Collier was one

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.330
of the most prolific literary

00:13:20.422 --> 00:13:22.490
forgers in English history.

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:25.130
He spent much of his later career

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:27.660
inserting fake notes and fake

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.780
documents into early Shakespeare

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:32.390
editions, and getting caught doing so.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.970
So historians today are not entirely sure

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:40.398
how much of what he wrote down in 1828

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:43.270
was a faithful record of what Piccini

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:45.480
actually performed, and how much was

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:49.250
tidied up, polished, or simply invented.

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:54.250
What is clear is the cast list, because

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:56.750
most of these characters appear again

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:59.030
and again in earlier descriptions.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:01.480
There is Punch himself.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:04.100
There is his wife Judy, hence

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.070
the name, "Punch and Judy".

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:07.780
There is their baby.

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:10.600
There is Toby the dog, who in earlier

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:12.970
shows was an actual live, performing

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.920
dog who sat on the front of the booth.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:16.400
There is the policeman.

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:18.890
There is a clown called Joey.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.500
There is Jack Ketch, the hangman.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:23.630
There is a hungry crocodile, who

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:24.870
is usually interested in Punch’s

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:27.730
sausages, which Punch has for

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:29.850
reasons that aren't entirely clear.

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:32.960
And in many versions, there is the

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:35.150
devil himself, who arrives at the

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:37.970
end to drag Mr Punch off to hell.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.640
The plot, what actually happens in a

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:45.060
Punch and Judy show, is famously violent.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:49.010
A typical show goes something like this.

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:52.340
Punch is left to look after the baby.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:53.820
The baby cries.

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:56.160
Punch loses patience and

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:57.590
throws it out of the window.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.310
Judy returns, understandably furious.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.360
She attacks Punch, Punch

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:05.610
attacks Judy, and before long

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.760
she too has been knocked aside.

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:10.480
Then society starts

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:12.520
arriving to restore order.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.660
A policeman comes to arrest Punch.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:17.550
Punch beats the policeman.

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:20.540
A hangman comes to execute Punch.

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:23.240
Punch tricks the hangman into

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:25.400
putting his own head in the noose.

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.490
A crocodile appears and

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:28.900
tries to steal the sausages.

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:30.610
Punch fights the crocodile.

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:33.850
And in some versions, when even the

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:35.960
devil comes to drag him down to hell,

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:39.210
Punch beats the devil too, cackling

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:40.730
while all of this is going on.

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:44.020
After each successful act of violence,

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:46.790
Punch leans forward and shouts his

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:48.990
catchphrase to the audience through

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:51.160
the swazzle: "That's the way to do it!"

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:54.000
And the audience, at least most

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.330
of the time, roars with laughter.

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.580
Now, by the early nineteenth

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:02.530
century, Mr Punch was already a

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:04.750
familiar street character in London

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:06.880
and other large English cities.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.000
The booth could be set up on

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.020
any corner, the show drew in a

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.260
crowd, and the professor passed

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:15.330
a hat at the end for tips.

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:19.040
It was rough, urban, working-class

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:21.080
entertainment — no longer the preserve

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.890
of Samuel Pepys and his beloved parmesan.

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:27.080
Then came two big changes.

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:29.770
The first was the railway.

00:16:30.530 --> 00:16:33.090
From the 1840s onwards, the

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.430
British railway network exploded.

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:38.200
For the first time, working

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:40.500
people in industrial cities had

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:43.430
cheap, fast access to the coast.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.500
Small fishing villages turned into

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:48.780
booming holiday resorts, as we'll

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:50.890
be talking more about in part three.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.860
And what did the new day-trippers want at

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:57.480
the seaside, alongside sand and ice cream?

00:16:58.010 --> 00:17:00.070
They wanted entertainment.

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:02.667
Punch professors were

00:17:02.667 --> 00:17:04.490
perfectly placed to provide it.

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:07.570
The booth, with its tall stripes of

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:09.870
red and white, was already portable.

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:11.970
The show was already short,

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:14.120
loud, and easy to follow.

00:17:14.830 --> 00:17:16.880
All a professor had to do was

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:19.170
pack up his cart, get on a train,

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:21.640
and set up shop on the beach.

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:24.980
By the late Victorian period, the

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:27.350
red-and-white striped puppet booth had

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:29.620
become one of the most recognisable

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:31.990
images of the British seaside.

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:36.140
The second big change was a magazine.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:41.020
In July 1841, two journalists

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:43.140
in London started a new

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:45.710
weekly satirical publication.

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:48.350
They needed a name, and

00:17:48.350 --> 00:17:49.680
they needed a mascot.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.810
They settled on the most famously

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:56.270
anarchic, disrespectful, authority-mocking

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:57.450
character they could think of,

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:59.790
and they called their magazine

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:02.290
"Punch", after Mr Punch himself.

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:06.260
The full title was "Punch, or the

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:09.150
London Charivari", and on its cover,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.370
week after week, Mr Punch and his

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:14.850
dog Toby looked out at the reader.

00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:17.910
The magazine started small.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:20.960
But by the 1850s it was selling

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:23.320
forty thousand copies a week, and

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:26.150
by 1910 over a hundred thousand.

00:18:27.220 --> 00:18:29.870
For Victorian Britain, this gave

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:32.630
Mr Punch a kind of double life.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.080
On the beach, he was a cheap,

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:37.250
knockabout puppet act for children.

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:40.230
In a smart London club, he was the

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:42.530
cover star of the country's most

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:44.500
prestigious satirical magazine.

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:46.370
The same hook-nosed

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:48.360
troublemaker stood for both.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:50.870
He had become a kind of national

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:53.970
symbol of British misrule: the small

00:18:53.980 --> 00:18:56.620
voice that mocks authority, breaks

00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:58.835
the rules, and gets away with it.

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.300
But the world that had made Punch

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:04.670
and Judy famous did not last.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:07.680
In its heyday, there were an estimated

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:11.290
400 Punch men, or professors, in Britain.

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:16.050
In 1939, a survey counted only

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:18.190
forty-four professional Punch men.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:21.520
And by the end of the twentieth century,

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:24.860
that number had fallen to around twelve.

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:28.080
There are some obvious reasons.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.450
From the 1960s onwards, cheap flights

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:33.550
and package holidays meant that British

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:36.740
holidaymakers increasingly jetted off for

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:38.950
the south of Spain rather than sitting

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:41.230
on a windy beach in northern England.

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.340
With fewer day-trippers, there was less

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:47.450
work for Punch professors, and fewer young

00:19:47.450 --> 00:19:49.560
people willing to spend years learning

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:51.920
the swazzle for a shrinking audience.

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:55.170
There is also the argument that the

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.780
style of the show, what was happening

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:00.780
on stage, was simply losing its appeal.

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:03.910
The most obvious problem is

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:05.330
the violence against women.

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:08.670
It is a violent show with a violent

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:10.840
male protagonist who beats his

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:12.850
wife with a stick, throws his

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:14.790
baby out of a window, and then

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:16.570
cackles "That's the way to do it!".

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:19.600
To a lot of people, that was

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:21.960
increasingly not funny, not

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:23.720
something they wanted to watch.

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:26.740
Domestic abuse charities have

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:28.830
argued that the show makes

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:30.900
light of violence in the home.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:33.790
Women's rights campaigners have questioned

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:36.250
what message it sends to small children.

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:39.720
And in some places, perfectly

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.580
understandably, this argument has won.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:46.820
A town council in South Wales removed

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:48.870
a Punch and Judy show from a local

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:50.910
festival on the grounds that it was,

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:53.940
in their words, "too violent and

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:56.220
contained inappropriate hitting".

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:59.150
And there was a recent survey of

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:01.490
two thousand British parents that

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:03.910
found around forty per cent thought

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:06.060
the traditional Punch and Judy was

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:08.220
too violent for their own children.

00:21:09.117 --> 00:21:10.627
The defenders of the show

00:21:10.767 --> 00:21:11.977
have their own arguments.

00:21:12.337 --> 00:21:15.727
They say it is satire, not instruction.

00:21:16.217 --> 00:21:18.747
They say Punch has always been

00:21:18.747 --> 00:21:21.217
a wicked character, and that the

00:21:21.217 --> 00:21:24.267
joke is on him, not on his victims.

00:21:24.957 --> 00:21:27.577
They point out that he ends up in hell

00:21:27.847 --> 00:21:30.257
in many versions of the script, which is

00:21:30.257 --> 00:21:32.027
hardly an endorsement of his behaviour.

00:21:32.877 --> 00:21:35.457
They also argue that children do not

00:21:35.457 --> 00:21:38.077
read puppet violence in the same way

00:21:38.187 --> 00:21:41.427
adults do: the violence is stylised,

00:21:41.817 --> 00:21:44.487
ridiculous, and obviously unreal.

00:21:45.387 --> 00:21:47.577
They suggest that this is nothing

00:21:47.667 --> 00:21:49.897
compared to many video games,

00:21:49.997 --> 00:21:52.337
where characters do much worse.

00:21:53.167 --> 00:21:56.027
And they argue that taking the violence

00:21:56.147 --> 00:21:58.557
out of Punch and Judy is a bit like taking

00:21:58.557 --> 00:22:00.857
the wolf out of Little Red Riding Hood.

00:22:01.297 --> 00:22:03.877
You can do it, but at the end

00:22:04.007 --> 00:22:05.567
it's not really the same story.

00:22:06.497 --> 00:22:08.827
This being said, there have been attempts

00:22:08.827 --> 00:22:11.947
to modernise, creating less violent

00:22:11.947 --> 00:22:14.537
versions of the show, or versions where

00:22:14.787 --> 00:22:17.597
Judy hits Punch as much as he hits her.

00:22:18.297 --> 00:22:19.887
The thing is, there really

00:22:19.887 --> 00:22:22.887
isn't a pure, original version.

00:22:23.387 --> 00:22:24.647
It has always changed.

00:22:24.947 --> 00:22:27.117
Punch looks different, what happens

00:22:27.127 --> 00:22:29.347
in the show has evolved, it has

00:22:29.347 --> 00:22:32.227
never been a single, fixed text.

00:22:32.567 --> 00:22:34.757
It is a tradition with a core set

00:22:34.757 --> 00:22:37.607
of characters, bent and rewritten in

00:22:37.647 --> 00:22:39.707
every generation that has performed it.

00:22:40.337 --> 00:22:42.247
So, what does the future

00:22:42.257 --> 00:22:44.027
hold for Punch and Judy??

00:22:44.577 --> 00:22:46.477
Well, it is hard to imagine the

00:22:46.477 --> 00:22:48.237
show returning to the centre

00:22:48.427 --> 00:22:49.847
of British popular culture.

00:22:50.637 --> 00:22:52.477
There are fewer professors than

00:22:52.477 --> 00:22:54.517
there used to be, and no great

00:22:54.527 --> 00:22:56.497
national movement to bring it back.

00:22:57.317 --> 00:22:59.947
If you are lucky, or unlucky, and you

00:22:59.947 --> 00:23:02.147
find yourself at a British seaside

00:23:02.157 --> 00:23:05.337
town, you might still stumble across the

00:23:05.367 --> 00:23:08.357
telltale red-and-white striped canopy

00:23:08.607 --> 00:23:11.357
of a Punch and Judy show, and hear

00:23:11.367 --> 00:23:13.866
the inimitable sound of the swazzle.

00:23:14.817 --> 00:23:17.007
If you ever see one, stop for a

00:23:17.007 --> 00:23:19.557
moment and have a look at Mr Punch.

00:23:20.157 --> 00:23:22.587
He has lasted three and a half centuries.

00:23:22.907 --> 00:23:24.207
He has outlived the king who

00:23:24.207 --> 00:23:26.794
first applauded him in 1662.

00:23:27.047 --> 00:23:28.777
He has outlived the magazine

00:23:28.981 --> 00:23:31.579
that borrowed his name in 1841.

00:23:31.897 --> 00:23:33.417
He has outlived generations of

00:23:33.417 --> 00:23:35.107
children who once stood on the

00:23:35.127 --> 00:23:37.467
sand, staring up at that striped

00:23:37.487 --> 00:23:40.007
booth, laughing at a puppet who did

00:23:40.067 --> 00:23:42.117
everything he was not supposed to do.

00:23:42.877 --> 00:23:45.677
Whatever happens to him next, that is,

00:23:46.117 --> 00:23:49.027
by any standard, a pretty remarkable run.

00:23:50.617 --> 00:23:52.657
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:23:52.657 --> 00:23:55.337
episode, the first part of our three-part

00:23:55.337 --> 00:23:57.957
mini-series on unusual British traditions.

00:23:58.337 --> 00:24:00.527
Next up we'll be moving into fox

00:24:00.527 --> 00:24:02.307
hunting, a very different type of

00:24:02.307 --> 00:24:04.017
entertainment that many people would

00:24:04.017 --> 00:24:05.857
say is not entertainment at all.

00:24:06.387 --> 00:24:08.767
And in part three, we'll tell the story

00:24:08.927 --> 00:24:11.497
of the rise and fall of the British

00:24:11.517 --> 00:24:13.917
seaside holiday, the place where Punch

00:24:13.917 --> 00:24:16.407
and Judy really became a cult tradition.

00:24:16.987 --> 00:24:18.817
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:18.817 --> 00:24:21.517
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:21.857 --> 00:24:24.687
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:24.687 --> 00:24:27.467
I'll catch you in the next episode.

